III. 
PREVENTION AND COUNSELING 

Prevention has been recommended as an important public health intervention to address the 
problem of alcohol dependence. Prevention helps by preventing the onset of alcohol use or 
problematic alcohol use (in case alcohol use has set in). 

Some of such strategies include: 
• Raising awareness and commitment among the general public regarding harmful effects 
of alcohol use 
• Health services’ response; 
• Community action; 
• Strict action against drunk-driving; 
• Regulating availability and marketing of alcohol; 
• Alcohol pricing policies; 
• Reducing the negative consequences of drinking and alcohol intoxication; 
• Reducing the public health impact of illicit alcohol and informally produced alcohol; 
• Monitoring and surveillance of sell of alcohol 

Counseling plays a key role in management of alcohol dependence both during the short term as well 
as long term phase of management. It has been described in greater details in the subsequent sections.

